(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["pharmacology-i-exam-3-pharm-e3-pulmonary-vignettes-version-2"]={"title":"Pulmonary Infections, Asthma and Chronic Obstructive Pulmonary Disease — Clinical Vignettes 2","category":"Pharmacology I","exam":"Exam 3","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 35-year-old woman started inhaled fluticasone a month ago and now has white plaques on her tongue and inner cheeks. Which adverse effect is this?","choices":["Anticholinergic dry mouth","Oral candidiasis (thrush)","Angioedema of the tongue","Neutralizing antibody reaction"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Oral candidiasis (thrush) is a listed adverse effect of inhaled corticosteroids, along with growth suppression in children at high doses and inhibition of the immune system. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 73"},{"q":"A 45-year-old man with a seizure disorder wants medication to help him quit smoking. Which agent should be avoided?","choices":["Varenicline","Bupropion","Nicotine patch","Nicotine gum"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Bupropion (sustained release, Zyban) is to be avoided in patients with seizure disorders and in patients taking monoamine oxidase inhibitors. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 105"},{"q":"A 64-year-old woman develops pneumonia on hospital day 3 after hip surgery. She has had no antibiotics or hospital stays in the past 90 days, no known colonization with resistant organisms, does not live in a care facility and is not immunosuppressed. Which drug is a limited-spectrum empiric choice for her?","choices":["Cefepime","Vancomycin","Piperacillin/tazobactam","Ceftriaxone"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Early onset (before day 5) with no risk factors for multidrug-resistant pathogens calls for limited-spectrum therapy: ceftriaxone, or levofloxacin or moxifloxacin, or ampicillin/sulbactam, or ertapenem. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"A 32-year-old woman in status asthmaticus is intubated after continuous nebulized albuterol, intravenous steroids and magnesium have failed. Which drug may be added now that she is intubated?","choices":["Ketamine","Salmeterol","Cromolyn sodium","Omalizumab"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Ketamine is used in status asthmaticus once the patient is intubated: a dissociative anesthetic (N-methyl-D-aspartate receptor antagonist) that increases catecholamine release and causes bronchodilation. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 99"},{"q":"A 45-year-old woman's asthma stays uncontrolled on high-dose inhaled corticosteroid with a long-acting beta-2 agonist, despite good inhaler technique and adherence. Her peripheral blood eosinophils are 400 per microliter. Which add-on drug fits her phenotype?","choices":["Cromolyn sodium","Mepolizumab","Theophylline","Ipratropium"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The anti-interleukin-5 biologics (mepolizumab, benralizumab, reslizumab) and the anti-interleukin-4 drug dupilumab are used for the eosinophilic phenotype (peripheral blood eosinophils above 150 per microliter), an add-on at the top step of asthma therapy. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 81"},{"q":"A 35-year-old woman being treated for anaphylaxis has received epinephrine and diphenhydramine, and famotidine is added. Which effects does famotidine block?","choices":["Rash, itching and hives","Bronchial smooth muscle spasm","Vasodilation and gastric acid secretion","Inflammatory mediator production"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Famotidine is a histamine H2 blocker: it blocks the systemic effects (vasodilation) and gastrointestinal effects (increased gastric acid secretion) of the massive histamine release. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"A 4-year-old girl with asthma will use a nebulizer at home because she cannot use a metered dose inhaler. What should her parents be told about the nebulizer?","choices":["It needs a strong, fast inhalation","It needs careful press-and-breathe timing","It gives a bigger dose, so more side effects","It can go without regular cleaning"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A nebulizer allows passive inhalation, which suits children and older adults who cannot use a metered dose inhaler, but it delivers a bigger dose overall and may lead to more side effects; it must also be cleaned regularly. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 92"},{"q":"A 70-year-old man with ventilator-associated pneumonia receives tobramycin as part of broad-spectrum coverage. Which pair of toxicities should his monitoring target?","choices":["Hearing and kidney function","Liver enzymes and uric acid","Color vision and visual acuity","Heart rate and blood glucose"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Aminoglycosides (gentamicin, tobramycin, amikacin) cause ototoxicity and nephrotoxicity; dose and frequency are adjusted on drug levels and depend heavily on renal function. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"A 30-year-old man with no medical problems has community-acquired pneumonia and will be treated at home. He has no risk factors for drug-resistant Streptococcus pneumoniae. Which drug is first line?","choices":["Doxycycline","Levofloxacin","Azithromycin","Cefpodoxime"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. With no risk of resistant Streptococcus pneumoniae, outpatient first-line therapy is a macrolide (azithromycin or clarithromycin); doxycycline or minocycline is second line. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"A 48-year-old man with a seizure disorder develops late-onset hospital-acquired pneumonia, and the team wants an antipseudomonal carbapenem. Which drug is the better choice for him?","choices":["Imipenem/cilastatin","Meropenem","Ertapenem","Cefepime"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Imipenem/cilastatin is used with caution in patients with a seizure history, while meropenem carries less risk for seizures; both are antipseudomonal carbapenems that require renal adjustment. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"A 27-year-old woman who is 12 weeks pregnant is diagnosed with active pulmonary tuberculosis. Which first-line drug should be avoided?","choices":["Isoniazid","Rifampin","Pyrazinamide","Ethambutol"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Pyrazinamide is to be avoided in pregnancy; it also causes hepatitis (monitor liver enzymes) and makes patients hyperuricemic. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"A 70-year-old woman with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease has mild symptoms but had two exacerbations last year, one needing hospitalization. Which regular treatment is suggested?","choices":["A long-acting muscarinic antagonist","A short-acting bronchodilator as needed","A regular long-acting beta-2 agonist","An inhaled steroid with a long-acting beta-2 agonist"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Mild symptoms with 2 or more exacerbations a year (one or more hospitalized) is group C: regular long-acting muscarinic antagonist treatment, with a short-acting beta-2 agonist for symptom relief as needed. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 102"},{"q":"A 58-year-old man who drinks heavily is starting isoniazid for tuberculosis. Which adverse effect is more likely because of his drinking?","choices":["Hepatitis","Lupus-like syndrome","Red-green color loss","Orange-colored urine"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Isoniazid hepatitis increases in alcoholics and with age, so liver injury is the risk his drinking raises. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"A 24-year-old woman with asthma now uses her albuterol inhaler several times a day and wakes most nights coughing. What does this pattern of albuterol use call for?","choices":["Switching her to levalbuterol","Stopping her albuterol","Adding salmeterol by itself","Adjusting her controller therapy"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Albuterol provides only short-term relief and should be used sparingly; excessive use indicates poor control and the need to adjust long-term controller therapy. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 98"},{"q":"A 66-year-old woman is admitted to a hospital ward with community-acquired pneumonia. Before her first antibiotic dose, which step is recommended?","choices":["Skip cultures and treat empirically","Draw blood and sputum cultures","Rely on a sputum Gram stain alone","Start antibiotics, then draw cultures"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. For hospitalized patients, blood and sputum cultures are obtained before antibiotics are given; outpatients, by contrast, do well with empiric antibiotics and are not routinely tested. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"A 58-year-old man with coronary artery disease plans to quit smoking with nicotine patches. Which risk calls for caution in him?","choices":["Seizures in a seizure disorder","Depression and suicidal thoughts","Tachyarrhythmias and hypertension","Nausea and sleep disturbance"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Nicotine replacement calls for caution in patients with cardiovascular problems because of the chance of tachyarrhythmias and hypertension. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 106"},{"q":"A 67-year-old woman with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease asks which vaccines she should receive. Which recommendation fits her?","choices":["Influenza vaccine in outbreak years","Pneumococcal vaccine instead of influenza","No vaccines until her disease is stable","Yearly influenza plus pneumococcal vaccine"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Patients with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease are at risk of serious illness and death from pneumonia caused by influenza; annual influenza vaccination is highly recommended, and they should also receive the pneumococcal vaccine. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 108"},{"q":"A 28-year-old man with persistent asthma asks to use salmeterol by itself as his one controller medicine. What should he be told?","choices":["It must be paired with an inhaled steroid","It can replace albuterol during attacks","It is safe alone if taken twice daily","It treats the airway inflammation itself"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Long-acting beta-2 agonists (formoterol, salmeterol) are not to be used alone for asthma; they are combined with an inhaled corticosteroid, as in salmeterol/fluticasone (Advair). — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 70"},{"q":"A 30-year-old woman has latent tuberculosis infection with no sign of active disease. Which drug is the one used by itself for prophylaxis?","choices":["Ethambutol","Pyrazinamide","Doxycycline","Isoniazid"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Isoniazid is used alone for prophylaxis and in combination therapy for active disease; it is bacteriostatic for latent and bactericidal for dividing bacteria. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"A 72-year-old man with heart failure has community-acquired pneumonia that will be treated at home. He had hives and throat swelling after penicillin. Which drug covers his resistance risk without a beta-lactam?","choices":["Levofloxacin","Amoxicillin-clavulanate","Cefuroxime","Azithromycin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. With risk factors for resistant Streptococcus pneumoniae (age over 65, heart failure), a respiratory fluoroquinolone (levofloxacin or moxifloxacin) can replace a beta-lactam plus a macrolide, and it is good for patients with a beta-lactam allergy. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"A 45-year-old man has finished the first two months of four-drug therapy for pulmonary tuberculosis. Which drugs continue for the next four months?","choices":["Isoniazid and ethambutol","Rifampin and pyrazinamide","Ethambutol and pyrazinamide","Isoniazid and rifampin"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Combination therapy is isoniazid, rifampin, ethambutol and pyrazinamide for 2 months, followed by isoniazid and rifampin for 4 months. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 35"}]};
